Helios DXVK fork: poll briefly before a worker or waiter sleeps

Conduit guest/windows/docs (382.1 windowed Heaven profile). Applied after
0013 by ci/windows/Build-Driver.ps1.

Under a hypervisor, waking a thread that sleeps on another vCPU costs an IPI
and a VM exit on the thread that wakes it. In the 382.1 profile of windowed
Heaven, HalpInterruptSendIpi was 2.7% of the app process. The app thread
dispatches ~17 CS chunks per frame into a CS thread that is idle 78% of the
time, so nearly every dispatch wakes a sleeping thread, and the present gate
(SynchronizeCsThread + syncSubmissions, ~180 us per frame) sleeps the app
thread and has the workers wake it again.

Each such wait now polls a lock-free indicator for up to
HELIOS_DXVK_SPIN_US microseconds (process environment; default 0 = off (384.1 A/B: no gain),
at most 1000) before it blocks on its condition variable as before:
- the CS worker, for a new chunk (m_heliosAdded, counted where chunks are
  queued);
- DxvkCsThread::synchronize / injectChunk(synchronize), for the CS counter;
- the submission thread, for a new submission (m_heliosSubmitPending,
  m_submitQueue's size);
- DxvkSubmissionQueue::synchronize (syncSubmissions), for an empty queue.
The predicates under the lock are unchanged: the poll only decides how long
a thread stays awake before it waits, never what it waits for.

